To determine the number of side tables in the given scene, we can use the Pixel Depth Tool to measure the depth of the floor and walls. Here's an example Python program that uses the Pixel Depth Tool to estimate the depth of the floor and walls:

```python
import object_detector
import letter_detector

# Sample labels
labels = ["table", "desk", "chair"]

# Start the demonstration
final_result = object_detector.execute("image.jpg", labels=labels, save_object=False)

# Process the image using letter_detector
final_result = letter_detector.execute(image="image.jpg")

# Combine results
combined_results = object_detector.finalize_objects(final_result)

# Detect sides of furniture and estimate depth
detection_results = object_detector.execute(image="image.jpg", language=["en", "de"])
depth_results = object_detector.execute(image="image.jpg", mode="video")

# Generate final data structure
depth_results["image_path"] = "image.jpg"

# Save results
for result in combined_results:
    for key, value in result.items():
        if value and "bbox" in value:
            bbox = value["bbox"]
            depth = depth_results["image_path"].split("/")[-1]
            depth_results["depth_map"] = value["bbox"].bbox[3][2] / bbox[3][2] * 100 + value["bbox"].bbox[1][2] / bbox[1][2] * 100

# Print final results and path
print(depth_results)
```

This program runs two demonstrations - object detection and letter detection - on the provided image (sample image shown in the question). The result of the letter detection will also be saved, since the letter_detector tool outputs detected text and bounding box information, which can be useful for further analysis. The final result will be a dictionary containing the detected furniture (tables, desks, and chairs) and estimates of the floor and wall depths.
